layout / \emph{Econometrica} and \emph{AER} journal convention.
Resolves the mixed-precision look of \code{"first_col"} (an
integer \code{n} row sharing the B column with two-decimal
coefficients).
}
\code{excel}, \code{flextable}, \code{word} and \code{tinytable} (in HTML, LaTeX
and Typst) merge the cells. The markdown output of \code{tinytable},
\code{gt} (with a warning), \code{clipboard} and \code{default} (console) keep
the \code{"first_col"} layout.
In \code{"first_col"} mode the \code{B} column aligns the fit-stat values
on the decimal mark with its coefficients. In \code{"merged"} mode the
fit-stat values move out of the \code{B} column, which aligns its
coefficients alone, and the merged values of a model align on the
decimal mark among themselves, one centered block under the model
(Excel centers each number as it is).
Under \code{nested = TRUE} the change rows carry their degrees of
freedom (\code{F-change} prints \code{7.29 (2, 28)}), which widens the \code{B}
column in the \code{"first_col"} layout; \code{"merged"} spans them over
the model's columns in the engines that merge.}

\item{layout}{How several models are laid out. \code{"columns"} (default)
sets them side by side, one group of columns per model. \code{"stacked"}
sets them one under the other, one panel per model: each panel is
the table of that model alone (its default columns, the confidence
interval included, its blocks and fit statistics), opened by a row
with its label, then by an Outcome row where \emph{Stacked layout} says.
See \emph{Multi-model semantics} and \code{\link[=table_stack]{table_stack()}},
which stacks tables built separately.}

\item{show_re}{Mixed models only: \code{TRUE} (default) renders the
variance components as a \verb{Random effects} block of rows, \code{FALSE}
suppresses it. See \link{table_regression_mixed}.}

\item{re_scale}{Mixed models only: \code{"sd"} (default) or \code{"variance"},
the scale of the random-effects rows. See \link{table_regression_mixed}.}

\item{re_columns}{Mixed models only: the cells of the random-effects
rows that are displayed, a subset of \code{c("est", "se", "ci")}. See
\link{table_regression_mixed}.}

\item{re_test}{Mixed models only: \code{"none"} (default), \code{"lrt"} or
\code{"rlrt"}, a per-term test of the variance components. See
\link{table_regression_mixed}.}

\item{re_ci}{Mixed models only: \code{"wald"} (default) or \code{"profile"},
the intervals of the random-effects rows of \code{lmer} and \code{glmer} fits.
See \link{table_regression_mixed}.}

\item{model_labels}{Per-model labels used as the \strong{column-group
spanner} above each model's sub-columns (console + gt /
flextable / tinytable / Excel / Word renderers). \code{NULL}
(default) resolves automatically; see \emph{Multi-model semantics}
for the full rule. A character vector of length
\code{length(models)} overrides. Refused (error) for a single
multinomial model: there the column groups are outcome
categories, relabeled via \code{outcome_labels}
(\link{table_regression_categorical}).}

\item{outcome_labels}{The \strong{Outcome body row}, which names the
outcome of each model under the column headers. \code{NULL} (default)
shows it when the models do not all have the same outcome and the
column headers do not name them: models named in the list or by
\code{model_labels}, or an outcome that several of the models share.
Otherwise the title (one outcome) or the column headers (the
outcomes of unnamed models, all different) name them, and the row
is left out. A character vector of length \code{length(models)} forces
the row with those values (the spanner stays as \code{"Model 1, ..."}
unless \code{model_labels} is also supplied), a single model included.
\code{FALSE} removes the row. With \code{layout = "stacked"}, the row opens
each panel, under the same rule, the labels of the panels taking
the place of the column headers; a vector gives each panel its label.
\code{\link[=table_stack]{table_stack()}} applies the rule to tables built separately, a table
built with \code{FALSE} taking no row. A multinomial model alone has no
Outcome row, its title naming the outcome; in a stack, its panel
takes one by this rule, the label once, across its columns. The
console, flextable, Word, Excel and tinytable (HTML, LaTeX, Typst)
print each label across the columns of its model. gt, which merges
no cells of the body, moves the row into the header: each label
spans the columns of its model, under the model's name. A stacked gt
table and the markdown grid of tinytable keep the label in the first
column of the model, so a long label widens that column.
For a single multinomial model, the labels of the category
spanners; see \link{table_regression_categorical}.}

\item{stars}{Significance asterisks. \code{FALSE} (default; the APA
Manual 7, Section 7.14, p. 204, prefers exact p-values) -- no stars. \code{TRUE} -- APA cutoffs
\code{c("*" = 0.05, "**" = 0.01, "***" = 0.001)}. A named numeric
vector specifies custom thresholds, e.g.
\code{c("+" = 0.10, "*" = 0.05, "**" = 0.01, "***" = 0.001)}.
With \code{output = "excel"} a marked estimate is written as text
(\code{"64.63***"} is not a number); the cells with no marker stay
numeric.}

\item{nested}{Whether to inject pairwise change-statistic rows
for adjacent models (M2 vs M1, M3 vs M2, ...). \code{FALSE}
(default) -- pure side-by-side display. \code{TRUE} -- requires
identical \code{nobs} and identical response variable across all
models. Under a non-classical \code{vcov}, each pair is tested by a
Wald test of the coefficients the current model adds, on that
model's matrix, shown as "Wald F-change" or "Wald
\eqn{\chi^2}{chi^2} (change)" by the regime of its coefficient
tests. See \emph{Hierarchical (nested) model comparison} for the rule,
the classes it covers and its small-sample caution.}

\item{digits}{Decimal places for general numeric tokens
(\code{b}, \code{beta}, \code{se}, \code{ci}, \code{t}, \code{f_change}, \code{lrt_change}, \code{ame},
\code{ame_se}, \code{weighted_nobs}; the Wald change statistics under a
robust \code{vcov} follow \code{f_change}) and for the term-level tests
(\code{partial_chi2}, \code{partial_f}). Default \code{2L}.}

\item{p_digits}{Decimal places for p-values (\code{p}, \code{ame_p},
\code{p_change}). APA-strict: leading zero stripped, \verb{<.001} (or
\verb{<.0001} etc. depending on \code{p_digits}) for small values.
Default \code{3L}; at least \code{1L}, as in the descriptive tables. Every
\verb{*_digits} argument is a whole number: a fraction is an error.}

\item{effect_size_digits}{Decimals for per-coefficient effect
sizes (\code{partial_f2}, \code{partial_eta2}, \code{partial_omega2}) and their
intervals. Default \code{2L}.}

\item{fit_digits}{Decimals for variance-explained / model-level
effect-size fit stats (\code{r2}, \code{adj_r2}, \code{r2_change},
\code{adj_r2_change}, \code{omega2}, \code{f2}, \code{f2_change}, \code{sigma},
\code{rmse}). Default \code{2L}.}

\item{ic_digits}{Decimals for information criteria and deviance
(\code{AIC}, \code{AICc}, \code{BIC}, \code{deviance}, and their \verb{_change} form).
Default \code{1L}.}

\item{decimal_mark}{The decimal mark, a single character: \code{"."} (the
default) or \code{","} in most styles; the Lancet style of
\code{\link[=spicy_style]{spicy_style()}} uses the middle dot. When
\code{","} is used, the CI bracket separator switches to \code{"; "}
automatically to avoid \code{"0,18 [0,07, 0,30]"} ambiguity.
With \code{output = "excel"}, a cell that shows one number is a number,
under any mark, which shows the decimal separator of the reader's
Excel; the mark applies to the text cells (\verb{<,001}). See \emph{Excel
output} in \link{spicy-package}.}

\item{align}{Numeric column alignment.
\code{"decimal"} (default) -- pre-pad cells so decimal marks line
up vertically (publication-style). For CI cells (\verb{[LL, UL]})
the left bracket, the LL decimal point, the comma separator,
the UL decimal point, and the right bracket are independently
aligned across rows.
\code{"center"} and \code{"right"} apply the same alignment to every
numeric column.}

\item{padding}{Non-negative integer giving the extra characters
added to each data column's auto-computed width when the
default \code{print} method renders the table. Default \code{0L}
(compact -- fits more models in the same console / page
width). Use \code{2L} (Stata-like) or \code{4L} for a more spacious
look. Headers stay centered above the data region regardless
of padding.}

\item{labels}{How the variables are named in the table: the outcome
in the title (and above its columns when the models have different
outcomes), a numeric term in its row, and a factor in its header.
Four forms:
\itemize{
\item \code{labels = FALSE} shows the names the model gives them (\code{bmi},
\code{age}, \code{sex}).
\item \code{labels = TRUE} shows each variable's \code{label} attribute (as set by
\code{haven}, \code{labelled}, or \code{\link[=label_from_names]{label_from_names()}}) when it is present,
not \code{NA}, and not empty, and the name otherwise: "Linear
regression: Body mass index", "Age (years)", "Sex:". The attribute
is read off the model frame, or off the data the model was fit on
where the model frame lost it. Factor levels, interactions, and
transformed terms (\code{log(income)}, \code{poly(age, 2)}) are not variables
and keep their names, as do the coefficient rows of
\code{factor_layout = "flat"}.
\item \code{labels = c(age = "Age (years)", sexMale = "Men")}, a named
character vector, gives the variables and rows it names these
labels. Every other variable follows \code{options(spicy.var_labels)}:
its name when the option is unset or \code{FALSE}, its \code{label}
attribute when it is \code{TRUE}.
\item \code{labels = var_labels(age = "Age in years")} gives the variables
and rows it names these labels, and every other variable its
\code{label} attribute, whatever the option says. \code{var_labels()} alone
is \code{labels = TRUE}.
}

The names are model identifiers, not display text: the outcome, the
term labels of \code{\link[stats:terms]{stats::terms()}} (\code{"age"}, \code{"sex"}, \code{"age:sex"}, a
factor's term naming its header, an interaction's term its row when
it has one row), and the coefficient names
(\code{"(Intercept)"}, \code{"sexMale"}; those of an \code{rms} fit in the spelling
of \code{lm()}, \code{"sexMale"} for rms's \code{"sex=Male"} and \code{"age:sexMale"}
for \code{"age * sex=Male"}), each naming one
row. They keep that form whatever the language of the table.
Classes with no \code{terms}
component (\code{nls()}, \code{sampleSelection::selection()}) are keyed on
their coefficient (or parameter) names alone. With several models,
a name is looked up in the terms of all of them and lands on the
rows where it exists. A name that matches nothing in the table warns
(class \code{spicy_unknown_name}), with the list of its terms, and is left
out, so one vector can still serve every table of a project. The
headers of the
subordinate blocks (\verb{Random effects}, \code{Thresholds},
\code{Zero-inflation}, ...) are set by the package and cannot be
relabeled here.

The default reads \code{options(spicy.var_labels)}, and a \code{TRUE} or
\code{FALSE} typed in the call wins over it. Any other value, \code{NULL}
included, is an error. A variable label documents the data file; a
published table carries the wording you set here. \code{note_labels} gives
the outcome a shorter label in the title.}

\item{note_labels}{The label a variable takes where the table names it
in a sentence, in the title and in the notes, in place of the one
\code{labels} gives it: a named character vector,
\code{note_labels = c(sex = "sex at birth")}, or a \code{\link[=var_labels]{var_labels()}} object,
which reads as one. \code{labels} can then name a row with a whole
question while the note says "Missing values removed: sex at birth
(2).". The rows, the column headers and the spanners keep the labels
of \code{labels}, and so do the title and the notes for a variable
\code{note_labels} does not name. A name that matches no column warns and
is left out, as in \code{labels}, and \code{NULL} (the default) renames nothing.
A note label
that another variable of the table shows is an error (class
\code{spicy_label_collision}): choose another one, or relabel the other
variable.}

\item{title, note}{Override or suppress the auto-built caption /
methodological footer. Three modes per argument, those \code{note} has in
every table function:
\itemize{
\item \code{NULL} (default): the package builds the standard caption
("Linear regression on \verb{<DV>}" / "Hierarchical linear
regression on \verb{<DV>}" / ..., the outcome named by its label in
\code{note_labels}, else in \code{labels}) and a methodological note, one
paragraph in the order of every table of the package: what
the model is (its family, design, scale of the coefficients,
reference categories, ...), the cases a univariable screen fits
on, the inference (VCV type, p-adjust method, ...), the glosses
of the blocks, then the abbreviations the column headers and the
rows of the fit statistics and of the random effects show
("AIC = Akaike information criterion"), defined as the style in
force asks (\code{spicy_style(abbreviations = )}; under \code{"jama"} they
open the note on a line of their own), and the sentences of
\code{note_add}.
Under \code{stars}, the legend of the stars follows on a line of its
own.
\item \code{FALSE}: the corresponding banner row is omitted from
every output engine, for \code{note} the legend of the stars
included. Use when the surrounding manuscript provides its own
caption / note.
\item character string (length 1): replaces the auto-built text.
A \code{title} is printed verbatim. A \code{note} replaces the general
note and is printed as typed (multi-line notes accepted via
embedded \code{"\\n"}) after the lead-in of the style, which a string
opening with a lead-in of its own does not repeat, the sentences
of \code{note_add} after it; the legend of the stars stays on its line
below, and \code{note = ""} keeps that legend alone.
}
Validation messages, the spanner row, and the in-body change-
stat rows are \emph{not} affected -- they belong to the table
structure, not to the banner.}

\item{note_add}{Sentences to add to the note: a character vector of one
or more strings, each printed as typed (no period is added) and joined
to the note by a space. They close its paragraph, after the
definitions of the abbreviations and before the legend of the stars,
in every output and in \code{attr(x, "note")}, and the sentences the table
writes stay. With a string in \code{note}, they follow it, so a wrapper can
add a house sentence to every table while one table replaces its note.
An \code{NA} or an empty string is an error, and so is \code{note_add} with
\code{note = FALSE}, which leaves no note to add to.}

\item{output}{Output type. \code{"default"} (a printable
\code{spicy_regression_table}); \code{"data.frame"} (the displayed table
with its numbers); \code{"long"} (one row per coefficient, broom's
names); \code{"tinytable"} / \code{"gt"} / \code{"flextable"} (rich-format
tables); \code{"excel"} (writes to \code{excel_path}); \code{"clipboard"}
(copies to system clipboard); \code{"word"} (writes flextable to
\code{word_path}).}

\item{excel_path}{File path for \code{output = "excel"}, ending in
\code{".xlsx"}, in a directory that exists. Default \code{NULL} (required when
\code{output = "excel"}).}

\item{excel_sheet}{Sheet name when writing to Excel. \code{NULL} (the
default) uses \code{"Regression"}.}

\item{clipboard_delim}{Field delimiter for
\code{output = "clipboard"}. Default \code{"\\t"} (tab-separated, pastes
cleanly into Excel / Google Sheets / Word). The clipboard
payload mirrors the Excel layout (title row, spanner row,
header, body, footer note) but is plain text -- horizontal
rules, cell merging, decimal alignment and the monospace font
cannot be encoded in delimited text and are therefore absent
from the paste. A cell holding the delimiter itself (a label
with a comma under \code{clipboard_delim = ","}, or any number under
\code{decimal_mark = ","}), a double quote or a line break is quoted
RFC 4180-style, so the grid survives whatever delimiter you
choose.

Paste behavior by target:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Excel / Google Sheets:} numerics are auto-detected and
right-aligned; text cells stay left-aligned. (P-values such
as \code{.005} get re-parsed as \code{0.005} by Excel's auto-format
-- to preserve the APA leading-zero-dropped display, prefer
\code{output = "excel"}.)
\item \strong{Word:} the paste is converted to a Word table; all
cells start left-aligned. Apply a Table Style
(Insert > Table > Design) for APA-style borders, and
set right-alignment on numeric columns
(Layout > Align Right). For a self-contained Word file
with borders and alignment pre-applied, use
\code{output = "word"} instead.
}}

\item{word_path}{File path for \code{output = "word"}, ending in
\code{".docx"}, in a directory that exists. Default
\code{NULL} (required when \code{output = "word"}). The Word table inherits
the flextable styling (Calibri font, APA borders, decimal-aligned
numerics) and adds Word-specific features: an auto-numbered
caption ("Table 1: ...", "Table 2: ...") via Word's \code{SEQ}
field so multiple \code{table_regression()} calls in one document
number consecutively; a re-printed header row on each page break;
row split prevention so a single coefficient row never wraps
across two pages; and an APA-styled note line (\verb{*Note.*} italic
prefix per APA Manual 7 §7.14).

\strong{R Markdown / Quarto:} for embedded use, prefer \code{output = "flextable"}
(returns the flextable object that knits to docx/HTML/PDF natively).
\code{output = "word"} writes a standalone .docx file, suited to scripted
exports rather than chunk-level rendering.}

\item{word_template}{Optional path to a custom .docx file used as the
template for \code{output = "word"}. The template's header, footer,
page size, margins, and named styles ("Table Caption" in
particular) are honored; the table is appended to the template
body. Useful for institutional templates with pre-set headers
("APA Style", "Manuscript Submission Template", etc.). Default
\code{NULL} (uses flextable's stock template).

\strong{Customizing the caption appearance:} the table caption is
tagged with the Word named style \code{"Table Caption"}. The visual
rendering (italic / bold / color / font) follows whatever that
style is set to in the docx template. The stock Word template
renders \code{"Table Caption"} in italic, the type of the APA table
title (APA Manual 7 section 7.11, p. 201), where the APA puts the
bold table number on its own line above it (section 7.10,
p. 200). For a different appearance (Nature-style
bold non-italic, APA-strict 2-line bold-number / italic-title,
etc.), edit the \code{"Table Caption"} style in a docx template and
pass it via \code{word_template = "your_template.docx"}. Style-based
delegation keeps the rendered caption consistent with the
surrounding document and lets editorial conventions (Nature,
APA-strict, journal-specific) be applied without modifying the
call site.}

\item{style}{A journal style: a theme name (\code{"jama"}, \code{"nejm"},
\code{"lancet"}, \code{"annals"}, \code{"apa"}, \code{"aer"}), a
\code{\link[=spicy_style]{spicy_style()}} object, or \code{NULL} (the default). A style only
changes DEFAULTS -- any argument you pass explicitly wins over it.
Set \code{options(spicy.style = )} for document-wide scope. A theme
covers numeric formatting, the word that opens the note and the
abbreviations the note defines, not full editorial conformity;
\code{?spicy_style} lists the exact rules each one encodes and the
official document they come from. An unknown name is an error.}
}
\value{
A \code{spicy_regression_table} object (a \code{data.frame}
subclass with classes \code{c("spicy_regression_table", "spicy_table", "data.frame")}) when \code{output = "default"}.
The result carries rendering attributes (\code{title}, \code{note},
\code{align}, \code{padding}) and provenance attributes (\code{outcome},
\code{model_ids}) consumed by the print method and the broom
methods. For other \code{output} values, returns the
format-specific object: \code{gt_tbl}, \code{flextable}, \code{tinytable}; for
\code{"data.frame"} the displayed table as a \code{data.frame} (see
\code{\link[=as.data.frame.spicy_regression_table]{as.data.frame.spicy_regression_table()}}); for \code{"long"} a \code{tbl_df}
(\code{model_id}, \code{outcome}, \code{term}, \code{estimate_type}, \code{estimate},
\code{std.error}, \code{conf.low}, \code{conf.high}, \code{statistic}, \code{df},
\code{df.residual} (the second df of an F test, \code{NA} elsewhere),
\code{p.value}, \code{test_type}, \code{nobs}, \code{r.squared}, \code{adj.r.squared}, ...,
and last \code{partial_type}, the hypothesis of a partial row's test,
\code{"II"} or \code{"III"}, \code{NA} elsewhere); the file
path, invisibly, for \code{"excel"} and \code{"word"}; the table, invisibly,
for \code{"clipboard"}. The two data frames carry the note as printed in
\code{attr(x, "note")}. With \code{layout = "stacked"}, the stacked table of
\code{\link[=table_stack]{table_stack()}}, whose value is described there.
}
\description{
Publication-ready coefficient table from one or more fitted
regression models: linear and generalized linear models, robust,
instrumental-variable, quantile and fixed-effects estimators,
mixed-effects and GEE models, ordinal, multinomial, count and
two-part models, survival models, survey-design fits, additive,
beta-regression and sample-selection models, and Bayesian fits,
39
model classes listed by \code{\link[=table_regression_models]{table_regression_models()}}. Several fits,
of one class or of several, go side by side in one table. For \code{lm}
and \code{glm}, it supports standardized coefficients
(\eqn{\beta}{beta}), average marginal effects (AME), partial
effect sizes (\emph{\eqn{f^2}{f^2}} / \emph{\eqn{\eta^2}{eta^2}} /
\emph{\eqn{\omega^2}{omega^2}} and partial \emph{F} for \code{lm}; partial
\emph{\eqn{\chi^2}{chi-squared}} or partial \emph{F} for \code{glm}), pseudo-\eqn{R^2}{R^2}
and deviance \eqn{R^2}{R^2} (\code{glm}), and a full vocabulary of variance
estimators
(classical / HC* / cluster-robust with Satterthwaite-corrected
df / bootstrap / jackknife). \code{glm} covers binomial / poisson /
Gamma / inverse.gaussian / quasi families with any link.
}
\section{Pages by model family}{
This page documents every argument. What is specific to one family of
models (its own arguments and the values the shared ones take for it)
is on the page of that family:

\itemize{
\item \link{table_regression_mixed}: \code{lmer}, \code{glmer}, \code{glmmTMB}, \code{lme}
and \code{gls} fits.
\item \link{table_regression_survival}: \code{coxph}, \code{survreg}, \code{cph} and
\code{flexsurvreg} fits, the RMST and risk-difference columns.
\item \link{table_regression_ordinal}: \code{polr}, \code{clm} and \code{clmm} fits.
\item \link{table_regression_categorical}: \code{multinom} and \code{mlogit} fits.
\item \link{table_regression_counts}: \code{zeroinfl} and \code{hurdle} fits, and
the zero-inflation, hurdle and dispersion blocks of \code{glmmTMB}.
\item \link{table_regression_survey}: \code{svyglm}, \code{svyolr} and \code{svycoxph}
fits.
\item \link{table_regression_bayesian}: \code{stanreg} and \code{brmsfit} fits.
\item \link{table_regression_robust}: \code{lm_robust}, \code{iv_robust}, \code{ivreg},
\code{tobit}, \code{rq} and \code{fixest} fits.
}

The other families (linear and generalized linear models, additive
models, beta regression and sample selection, and the \code{rms} fits)
follow the rules of this page. GEE fits are described in
\code{\link[=table_regression_models]{table_regression_models()}}, which lists every supported class.
}

\section{Vocabulary tokens}{
Two vector arguments -- \code{show_columns} and \code{show_fit_stats} --
accept named tokens that select \strong{what} to display and in
\strong{what order}. All tokens are lowercase
(snake_case for compound tokens). Group tokens
(\code{"all_b"}, \code{"all_ame"}, ...) expand to a fixed vector of
atomic tokens; see \code{show_columns} below.
\subsection{\code{show_columns} -- per-coefficient columns}{

Each token = one displayed column.

\itemize{
\item Coefficient family: \code{"b"}, \code{"beta"} (standardized),
\code{"se"}, \code{"ci"}, \code{"t"}, \code{"p"}.
\item Marginal effects: \code{"ame"}, \code{"ame_se"}, \code{"ame_ci"},
\code{"ame_p"}. \code{"p"} always refers to the B-coefficient
p-value; for the AME-specific p-value use \code{"ame_p"}.
\item Partial effect sizes -- \code{lm} only: \code{"partial_f2"},
\code{"partial_eta2"}, \code{"partial_omega2"}, each with a paired
\verb{_ci} companion (\code{"partial_f2_ci"}, ...).
\item Term-level test -- \code{lm}, \code{glm} and mixed-effects models:
\code{"partial_chi2"} and its twin \code{"partial_f"}, the term-level
test. By default (\code{partial_type = "II"}) each term is tested
under the marginality-respecting hypothesis that excludes any
higher-order interaction containing it (the
\code{car::Anova(type = 2)} convention), so the value does not
depend on the factor coding. \code{partial_type = "III"} keeps the
interactions and tests the term in a sum-to-zero coding (see
\code{partial_type}). For \code{glm} the term-level test is
the F that \code{anova(reduced, full, test = "F")} reports when the
dispersion is estimated, the likelihood-ratio chi-square
otherwise: \code{"partial_f"},
\eqn{F = (\Delta D / q) / \hat\phi}{F = (Delta D / q) / phi}
on \eqn{(q, df)} degrees of freedom, \eqn{q} the term's and
\eqn{df} the residual degrees of freedom of the model, with
\eqn{\hat\phi}{phi} the dispersion \code{summary()} estimates, for
gaussian, Gamma, inverse Gaussian, Tweedie,
\code{MASS::negative.binomial(theta)} fitted by \code{glm()} and the quasi
families (the value \code{car::Anova(type = 2, test.statistic = "F", error.estimate = "dispersion")} reports; car's default Pearson
estimate differs by the IRLS convergence error only, and
\code{drop1(test = "F")} divides by the deviance dispersion instead);
\code{"partial_chi2"}, the likelihood-ratio chi-square (Long & Freese
2014 Sections 3.2.2, 3.2.4), for binomial, Poisson and
\code{MASS::glm.nb}. For \code{lm}, \code{"partial_f"} is the F of
\code{car::Anova(type = 2)} (\code{type = 3} under \code{partial_type = "III"}),
the F the variance-explained partials are built on, so a gaussian \code{glm} and its \code{lm()} twin print the same
value. A token is refused when no model of the table carries it,
and when a model carries the other one while that one is not
requested (each test has its own column: ask for both). Both come
from the refits and the model's own dispersion (the residual
variance of an \code{lm}) whatever \code{vcov}.
For the mixed models, \code{"partial_chi2"} is the Wald chi-square
of the same hypothesis; see \link{table_regression_mixed}. Rendered as \code{value (df)} to
disambiguate factor terms (k-1 df) from numeric terms (1 df),
and the partial F as \code{F (q, df)} with both of its degrees of
freedom, \code{3.45 (2, 28)}, as Stata and SAS report them: each cell
carries the residual df of its own model, and the note defines
the statistic only. The console and the rich outputs (tinytable,
gt, flextable, Word, the clipboard) print the test in one column
under the statistic's header, and Excel writes the statistic as a
number with its df in the number format (\verb{0.00" (2, 28)"}).
\code{output = "data.frame"} and \code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}} keep the statistic
and its df as numbers, in the statistic's column, then
\code{"<column>: df"} and, for the F, \code{"<column>: residual df"}.
\code{tidy()} has them in \code{df} and \code{df.residual}, and \code{inline()} cites
the statistic alone.
\item Variance explained per fit -- \code{lm} only: \code{"r2"},
\code{"adj_r2"}. Like \code{"n"}, these are \emph{per-fit} columns: they are
populated by \code{\link[=table_regression_uv]{table_regression_uv()}} screens, where each
predictor block is its own model and the \eqn{R^2}{R^2}
answers "how much of the outcome does this predictor explain
on its own". A model whose \eqn{R^2}{R^2} is a single
model-level number drops the column and reports it in the
fit-statistics rows instead (the \code{"r2"} / \code{"adj_r2"} tokens of
\code{show_fit_stats}, on by default for \code{lm}). Refused for classes
with no least-squares variance partition (\code{glm}, \code{coxph},
...), where only competing pseudo-\eqn{R^2}{R^2} measures
exist: name the one you want in \code{show_fit_stats}.
\item Sample columns: \code{"n"} -- per-row N, populated by
\code{\link[=table_regression_uv]{table_regression_uv()}} screens (each predictor block is its
own fit); models without per-row N data drop the column.
\code{"n_events"} -- outcome event counts as \code{events/N}, per
factor level (reference row included) and model totals on
continuous rows, computed on each model's own estimation
sample (STROBE item 16's "data behind the measures of association";
NEJM-style \verb{no. of events/total no.}). Binary (binomial)
outcomes and right-censored \code{coxph} fits -- other models
raise \code{spicy_invalid_input}.
\item Survival estimands -- \code{coxph} and \code{survreg}: \code{"rmst"},
\code{"rmst_se"}, \code{"rmst_ci"}, \code{"rmst_p"} (restricted-mean-
survival-time difference over \verb{[0, tau]}) and
\code{"risk_diff"}, \code{"risk_diff_se"}, \code{"risk_diff_ci"},
\code{"risk_diff_p"} (cumulative-incidence difference at
\code{at_time}), by g-computation from the fitted model with
bootstrap inference. The horizon arguments are required;
see \code{tau} / \code{at_time} and \link{table_regression_survival}.
}

\strong{Group tokens} (presets) expand to a fixed atomic vector
before validation:

\itemize{
\item \code{"all_b"}         -> \code{c("b", "se", "ci", "p")}
\item \code{"all_b_compact"} -> \code{c("b", "se", "p")}
\item \code{"all_b_full"}    -> \code{c("b", "se", "ci", "t", "p")}
\item \code{"all_beta"}      -> \code{c("b", "beta", "se", "ci", "p")}
\item \code{"all_ame"}       -> \code{c("ame", "ame_se", "ame_ci", "ame_p")}
\item \code{"all_ame_compact"} -> \code{c("ame", "ame_p")}
\item \code{"all_f2"} / \code{"all_eta2"} / \code{"all_omega2"} -> \verb{partial_*}
+ its \verb{_ci} companion.
}

Mix groups and atomic tokens:
\code{show_columns = c("all_b", "ame", "ame_p")}. Duplicates after
expansion are deduplicated; the order of tokens controls the
order of the displayed columns. If \code{standardized != "none"} and
\code{"beta"} is not already requested, it is auto-injected after
\code{"b"}. Asking for \code{"beta"} while \code{standardized = "none"} raises
\code{spicy_invalid_input}.

\strong{Default} (\code{show_columns = NULL}) is context-aware:
\code{"all_b"} for a single model (the layout of the APA Manual 7
sample regression tables, Tables 7.16 and 7.17, pp. 219-220),
\code{"all_b_compact"} for two or more models (CI dropped to fit the
side-by-side layout; restore it explicitly when needed).

\strong{Fit statistics alone} (\code{show_columns = "none"}): no coefficient
row or column. Each model has one column, headed by its label (the
outcome, "Model 1", a name of the list or \code{model_labels}, as for the
column groups of the full table; "Model 1" for a single model), and
the rows are the fit statistics of \code{show_fit_stats}, in its order,
the change rows of \code{nested = TRUE} included. The title is the one of
the full table; the note keeps what describes the models and their
fit, and leaves out what describes coefficients. Models with
different outcomes get an Outcome row when the column headers do not
name them (\code{outcome_labels = FALSE} removes it). \code{"none"} goes alone, and
\code{show_fit_stats = FALSE} is refused with it, as is \code{layout = "stacked"}; an empty \code{show_columns} (\code{character(0)}, \code{FALSE}) is an
error that points to \code{"none"}. The arguments that act on coefficients
only are ignored, with one \code{spicy_ignored_arg} warning naming those
set to other than their default: \code{ci_level}, \code{ci_method}, \code{tau},
\code{at_time}, \code{standardized}, \code{exponentiate}, \code{ame_type}, \code{p_adjust},
\code{keep}, \code{drop}, \code{show_intercept}, \code{show_thresholds},
\code{show_components}, \code{intercept_position}, \code{factor_layout},
\code{reference_style}, \code{reference_label}, \code{fit_stats_layout}, \code{show_re},
\code{re_scale}, \code{re_columns}, \code{re_test}, \code{re_ci}, \code{stars},
\code{effect_size_digits}, and \code{vcov}, \code{cluster} and \code{boot_n} unless
\code{nested = TRUE} (where a robust \code{vcov} gives the Wald change rows).
An information criterion (AIC, BIC, deviance, ...) shown for models
fitted to different numbers of observations or to different outcomes
raises a \code{spicy_caveat} warning: its differences compare nothing.
\code{tidy()} returns no row, \code{glance()} one row per model, and
\code{inline()} cites a statistic of a model: \code{inline(tbl, "aic", model = 2)}. \code{\link[=table_regression_uv]{table_regression_uv()}} refuses the token.
}

\subsection{\code{show_fit_stats} -- model-level rows below the coefficients}{

\itemize{
\item Counts: \code{"nobs"}, \code{"weighted_nobs"}, \code{"n_events"} (Cox
models: number of events, reported alongside \code{n} per the
field convention; blank for other classes). \code{"nobs"} and the
BIC follow the engine's \code{\link[stats:nobs]{stats::nobs()}}, which for a weighted
\code{polr} or \code{clm} fit is the sum of the weights.
\item Variance explained (\code{lm} only): \code{"r2"}, \code{"adj_r2"},
\code{"omega2"}.
\item \eqn{R^2}{R^2} outside least squares (\code{glm}, \code{polr}, \code{clm},
\code{multinom} and \code{rms::lrm} fits): \code{"pseudo_r2_tjur"} (Tjur 2009;
binomial models only), \code{"pseudo_r2_mcfadden"} (McFadden 1974),
\code{"pseudo_r2_nagelkerke"} (Nagelkerke 1991) and \code{"r2_deviance"}
(Cameron & Windmeijer 1997; \code{glm} families other than the
binomial). What each one computes, and which a family shows by
default, is in \emph{R-squared of glm and categorical models} below.
\item Residual scale: \code{"sigma"}, \code{"rmse"}. \code{"sigma"} is the lm
\eqn{\hat{\sigma}}{sigma-hat}. For glm and the GLM-like \code{fixest}
fits, it is the square root of the dispersion (so a gaussian glm
reads as lm), and 1 when the family fixes it (binomial, Poisson).
For \code{survreg}, it is the scale \eqn{\sigma}{sigma} of
\eqn{\log T = X'\beta + \sigma W}{log T = X'beta + sigma W} for the
distributions fitted on \eqn{\log T}{log T} (the standard deviation
of \eqn{\log T}{log T} for the log-normal), and of
\eqn{T = X'\beta + \sigma W}{T = X'beta + sigma W} for those that
model \eqn{T}{T} itself (gaussian, logistic, extreme value, t),
blank when strata give one scale per stratum. For \code{glmmTMB}, it is
the same square root for the gaussian, Gamma and Tweedie families,
and the fixed 1 of Poisson and binomial. It is blank for the
negative-binomial and beta fits below, whose dispersion has its
own row, and for the other \code{glmmTMB} families, whose \code{sigma()} is
a family-specific parameter. For \code{mgcv::gam} and \code{bam}, a glm
family follows the glm rule; an extended family shows the square
root of its scale only when the GAM estimates one (\code{tw()}), and is
blank otherwise (an estimated \code{nb()}, \code{betar()}, \code{scat()}, \code{ziP()},
\code{ocat()}):
mgcv fixes their scale at 1.
\item Survey designs (\code{survey::svyglm} only): \code{"eff_p"}, the
effective number of parameters of the design (the first element
of survey's \code{extractAIC}, the sum of the Rao-Scott eigenvalues).
Opt-in and distinct from \code{"aic"}, which reports the design-based
AIC of Lumley & Scott (2015). Blank for other classes.
\item Bayesian fits only: \code{"r2_bayes"} (posterior-median
Bayesian \eqn{R^2}{R^2}, Gelman et al. 2019 -- in the
all-Bayesian default), \code{"elpd_loo"} and \code{"looic"} (PSIS-LOO
expected log predictive density and its deviance-scale twin,
Vehtari et al. 2017; opt-in, a few seconds per model; the
footer discloses the elpd standard error), and \code{"waic"}
(Watanabe-Akaike; PSIS-LOO is generally preferred). See
\link{table_regression_bayesian}.
\item Negative-binomial dispersion. NB2 fits (\code{MASS::glm.nb},
\code{lme4::glmer.nb}, \code{fixest::fenegbin}, \code{mgcv::gam} with \code{nb()},
\code{pscl::zeroinfl} and \code{pscl::hurdle} with \code{dist = "negbin"}, whose
theta is that of the count part, and \code{glmmTMB} with \code{nbinom2} or
\code{truncated_nbinom2}): \code{"theta"}
(\eqn{V = \mu + \mu^2/\theta}{V = mu + mu^2/theta}) and \code{"alpha"}
(\eqn{= 1/\theta}{= 1/theta}, the Stata \code{nbreg} convention).
NB1 fits (\code{glmmTMB} with \code{nbinom1} or \code{truncated_nbinom1}):
\code{"delta"} (\eqn{V = \mu(1 + \delta)}{V = mu(1 + delta)}, the
\eqn{\delta}{delta} of Stata's \verb{nbreg, dispersion(constant)}).
\item Beta precision: \code{"phi"}. In beta regression
(\code{betareg::betareg}, \code{mgcv::gam} with \code{betar()}, and \code{glmmTMB}
with \code{beta_family()}), \eqn{Var(y) = \mu(1-\mu)/(1+\phi)}{Var(y) =
    mu(1-mu)/(1+phi)} (Ferrari & Cribari-Neto 2004; higher
\eqn{\phi}{phi} = less dispersion). For \code{glmmTMB} with
\code{betabinomial()} or \code{ordbeta()}, \eqn{\phi}{phi} is the precision
parameter of the beta part. A constant precision is
back-transformed from its link, so \code{y ~ x | 1} reports the
same \eqn{\phi}{phi} as \code{y ~ x}.
\item For these dispersion rows (\code{"theta"}, \code{"alpha"}, \code{"delta"},
\code{"phi"}), a column whose model lacks the parameter is blank, so a
Poisson model can stand beside an NB2 model, or NB1 beside NB2.
A parameter fixed in the call is not an estimate and has no row:
\code{MASS::negative.binomial(theta)} in \code{glm()}, mgcv's
\code{negbin(theta)}, and \code{nb(theta)} or \code{betar(theta)} with
\code{theta > 0}. Such a negative-binomial model keeps the glm rule for
\code{"sigma"}. A negative-binomial \code{glmer()} always shows its theta,
which lme4 counts in the degrees of freedom of the model: a
\code{glmer.nb()} fit, its \code{update()} refits, and a \code{glmer()} with
\code{negative.binomial(theta)}.
For \code{mgcv::gam} and \code{bam}, theta and phi are estimated with the
smoothing parameters (REML by default), so they differ from the
maximum-likelihood values of \code{glm.nb()}, betareg or \code{glmmTMB}.
\code{method = "ML"} gives those.
A token is refused only when no model in the table has the
parameter, or when a model of that family has dispersion
covariates (a \code{glmmTMB} \code{dispformula}, or a betareg \code{y ~ x | z}),
so the parameter is not a single number.
\item GEE (\code{geepack::geeglm} only): \code{"qic"} and \code{"qicu"} -- the
quasi-likelihood information criteria (Pan 2001; QIC for
working-correlation choice, QICu for covariate choice) via
\code{\link[geepack:QIC]{geepack::QIC()}}, computed only when requested (QIC silently
refits the independence working model); \code{"scale"} -- the
estimated scale (dispersion) parameter, shown only when the
fit estimated it (blank under \code{scale.fix = TRUE}, matching
geepack's own summary); \code{"max_cluster_size"} -- the largest
cluster in the estimation sample (on by default with
\code{"n_groups"}, which renders the \verb{N (<id>)} cluster count).
Cluster statistics count the clusters geepack itself used
(\code{geese$clusz}, consecutive runs of \verb{id =} values), so they
always describe the displayed sandwich inference.
Refused when no model in the table is a geeglm fit; the
likelihood-based tokens (\code{"aic"}, pseudo-\eqn{R^2}{R^2}, ...)
are refused in return for all-GEE tables -- quasi-likelihood
has no likelihood.
\item Absorbed fixed effects (\code{fixest} fits, and \code{estimatr} fits
built with \verb{fixed_effects =}): \code{"fixed_effects"} renders a
\verb{Fixed effects:} block at the top of the fit statistics --
one Yes / No row per absorbed factor (the \code{etable} / \code{esttab}
convention), blank cells for models without the concept in
mixed tables -- and is on by default for both.
Varying-slope-only factors (\code{Origin[[x]]}) absorb no
intercept: they read No when another model absorbs that
factor, and contribute no row otherwise.
\code{"within_r2"} is the FE-partialed within
R-squared (a default for \code{feols}, except an IV one; opt-in
for \code{estimatr};
GLM-family fixest fits report fixest's McFadden \code{pr2}
instead). Both tokens are refused when no model in the table
absorbs fixed effects.
\item Effect size: \code{"f2"}.
\item Information criteria: \code{"aic"}, \code{"aicc"}, \code{"bic"},
\code{"deviance"} (lowercase like every other token; the rendered
row labels stay \code{AIC} / \code{AICc} / \code{BIC}). The AIC and BIC of a
\code{fixest} model are those of its \code{lm()}, \code{glm()} or
\code{MASS::glm.nb()} twin with the fixed effects as dummies: \code{glm()}'s
log-likelihood for a Gaussian, Gamma or inverse-Gaussian \code{feglm()},
with the residual variance, dispersion or theta counted, which
fixest's \code{AIC()} leaves out. A Gaussian \code{femlm()} follows the same
rule; Poisson, binomial and IV fits keep fixest's values. A lean
fit (\code{lean = TRUE}) that lost what they need, its weights or the
fitted values of a Gamma or inverse-Gaussian \code{feglm()}, shows no
AIC, BIC or nested rows. A survey-design fit has no likelihood:
\code{"deviance"}, \code{"bic"}, \code{"aicc"}, the pseudo-R-squared tokens and
\code{"r2_deviance"} are refused for a table of design fits, and \code{"aic"}
when no model of the table is a \code{svyglm}.
\item Log-likelihood: \code{"log_lik"}, the value of \code{\link[stats:logLik]{stats::logLik()}},
from which the AIC row is computed. It is the restricted
log-likelihood of a model fitted by REML, and for a binomial \code{glm()}
it is \code{logLik()}'s value whatever the layout of the data: it includes
the binomial coefficients of a grouped response and rounds
non-integer weights, unlike the pseudo-\eqn{R^2}{R^2} rows. A column
whose model has no likelihood (a quasi family, a survey design, GEE,
two-stage least squares) shows a dash; the token is refused when no
model of the table has one, and for Bayesian fits.
\item Cox models (\code{survival::coxph} and \code{survey::svycoxph}):
\code{"concordance"}, Harrell's C, the value \code{summary()} reports. When
the row is shown, the concordance leaves the note, which otherwise
gives it with its standard error. \code{rms::cph} fits have no
concordance row or note.
\item Change-stats for hierarchical comparison
(active under \code{nested = TRUE}; see \emph{Hierarchical
comparison} below): \code{"r2_change"}, \code{"adj_r2_change"},
\code{"f_change"}, \code{"f2_change"}, \code{"lrt_change"},
\code{"aic_change"}, \code{"aicc_change"}, \code{"bic_change"},
\code{"deviance_change"}, \code{"p_change"}. The differences, current
model minus previous (\code{"r2_change"}, \code{"adj_r2_change"},
\code{"f2_change"}, and the AIC, AICc and BIC changes), print with an
explicit sign (\code{+0.07}, \code{-9.4}) in every output. The test
statistics and \code{"p_change"} are never signed. The test statistics
carry their degrees of freedom, as the partial F cell does:
\code{12.38 (1, 29)} for an F, the number of added parameters and the
residual df of the larger model (the denominator df for the Wald F
of a robust \code{vcov}), \code{9.12 (1)} for a chi-square. A df that is not
a whole number (the HTZ denominator under \code{"CR2"}, the df of a
smooth term) prints with one decimal, \code{174.77 (2, 0.8)}.
The rich outputs print the same cell, \code{inline()} cites the
statistic alone, \code{output = "data.frame"} keeps it as a number,
and Excel writes the number with the df in its number format.
\code{"deviance_change"}
is the drop in deviance (previous minus current), positive when
the larger model fits better, and is not signed either.
}

Default (resolved when \code{NULL}) is class-aware: lm and
\code{lm_robust} fits get
\code{c("nobs", "r2", "adj_r2")}; glm, \code{polr}, \code{clm}, \code{multinom} and
\code{rms::lrm} fits get \code{"nobs"}, the \eqn{R^2}{R^2} rows of their family
(see \emph{R-squared of glm and categorical models}) and \code{"aic"};
mixed lm + glm sets union both groups (the renderer per-row
en-dashes the inappropriate cell); Cox fits get
\code{c("nobs", "n_events", "aic")}; GEE fits get
\code{c("nobs", "n_groups", "max_cluster_size")} (the cluster
structure, Stata \code{xtgee}-header style). When \code{nested = TRUE}, the
class-aware default is extended with change tokens
(\code{c("r2_change", "f_change", "p_change")} for lm,
\code{c("lrt_change", "p_change")} for glm, \code{c("f_change", "p_change")}
for a glm whose dispersion is estimated). The order of tokens in
\code{show_fit_stats} controls the order of the rows.
}
}

\section{R-squared of glm and categorical models}{
Outside least squares there is no variance to partition, and several
\eqn{R^2}{R^2} measures compete. They answer different questions and
give different numbers on the same fit, so the row label names the
measure, and a report should too (Long & Freese 2014, p. 126). A
threshold quoted for one measure, such as 0.2 to 0.4 for McFadden's,
does not carry over to another (Hemmert et al. 2018).

\itemize{
\item \code{"pseudo_r2_tjur"}, Tjur's coefficient of discrimination: the
mean predicted probability of the observations with the event
minus that of the observations without it (Tjur 2009). Binomial
models only.
\item \code{"pseudo_r2_mcfadden"}, McFadden's \eqn{R^2}{R^2}:
\eqn{1 - \log L / \log L_0}{1 - log L / log L0}, the proportional
reduction of the log-likelihood from that of the intercept-only
model, \eqn{L_0}{L0} (McFadden 1974).
\item \code{"pseudo_r2_nagelkerke"}, Nagelkerke's \eqn{R^2}{R^2}: Cox and
Snell's \eqn{1 - (L_0 / L)^{2/n}}{1 - (L0 / L)^(2/n)} divided by its
largest possible value, \eqn{1 - L_0^{2/n}}{1 - L0^(2/n)}
(Nagelkerke 1991). It is the max-rescaled R-square of SAS and the
Nagelkerke R square of SPSS.
\item \code{"r2_deviance"}, the deviance \eqn{R^2}{R^2}:
\eqn{1 - D / D_0}{1 - D / D0}, the proportional reduction of the
deviance from that of the intercept-only model (Cameron & Windmeijer
1997). It lies between 0 and 1, and within a family it cannot
decrease when a predictor is added. For a gaussian \code{glm()} with the
identity link it is the \eqn{R^2}{R^2} of \code{lm()}. For \code{mgcv::gam}
and \code{bam} fits of the same families (and of mgcv's \code{nb()} and
\code{tw()}), it is the deviance explained of their \code{summary()}, as a
proportion. A binomial or quasi-binomial GAM, and those of mgcv's
other extended families, have none.
}

A GAM shows none of the other \eqn{R^2}{R^2} rows of this section, and
they are refused for a table of GAMs. mgcv reports only an adjusted
\eqn{R^2}{R^2}: it is the \code{"adj_r2"} row of a GAM, in a table of GAMs
or beside an \code{lm()}. The \code{"r2"} row of a GAM is dashed beside other
models, and refused for a table of GAMs.

The intercept-only model keeps the offset and the weights of the
fitted model. For a \code{MASS::glm.nb()} fit it re-estimates theta in
McFadden's and Nagelkerke's \eqn{R^2}{R^2}, as Stata's \code{nbreg} does,
and keeps the theta of the fitted model in the deviance
\eqn{R^2}{R^2} (Cameron & Windmeijer 1997). By default each model
shows the rows of its family:

\itemize{
\item A logit on a binary outcome (0/1, a factor or a logical), a
\code{glm()} or a binary \code{rms::lrm()}: Tjur's \eqn{R^2}{R^2}, then
McFadden's. Tjur's reads on the scale of the predicted
probabilities; McFadden's cannot decrease when a predictor is
added. An \code{lrm} fitted without \code{y = TRUE} reads its outcome from
the data, as a \code{glm()} does; when the data are gone, its Tjur's
cell is a dash and the row leaves its default.
\item The other binomial models (probit, cloglog, a grouped
response), Poisson and negative binomial models, \code{polr()},
\code{clm()}, \code{multinom()} and an ordinal \code{rms::lrm()}: McFadden's
\eqn{R^2}{R^2},
which depends less than Cox and Snell's or Nagelkerke's on the
proportion of events (Menard 2000) and is the Pseudo R2 of Stata's
estimation output (Long & Freese 2014, p. 126).
\item The continuous families of \code{glm()} (gaussian, Gamma, inverse
Gaussian, Tweedie): the deviance \eqn{R^2}{R^2}. McFadden's and
Nagelkerke's are refused for them: the log-likelihood of a density
changes with the unit of the outcome, so the same model gets other
values, even negative ones, when the outcome is rescaled. For a
continuous outcome, the likelihood-ratio index can take large
values, positive or negative (Cameron & Windmeijer 1997, p. 333).
\item The quasi families have no likelihood and no default row.
}

A table of several models shows the union of their rows. A row is
filled for every model that defines the measure, and dashed for the
others. Nagelkerke's \eqn{R^2}{R^2} is shown on request only: its
rescaling is ad hoc and its values run high (Allison 2014). The
deviance \eqn{R^2}{R^2} is available on request
for Poisson, negative binomial and quasi models, where McFadden's is
lower (Cameron & Windmeijer 1997). For a \code{MASS::glm.nb()} fit both
deviances take the theta of the fitted model. It is refused for
binomial, ordinal and multinomial models, where it equals McFadden's.

A binomial model is measured on its individual trials, whatever the
layout of its data: a \code{cbind(successes, failures)} response, a
proportion with the trials as \code{weights}, and 0/1 rows weighted by
counts give the McFadden, Nagelkerke and Tjur \eqn{R^2}{R^2} of the
same model fitted to the expanded 0/1 rows. The likelihood is that of
the trials, n is their number, and Tjur's means are weighted by them.
For every family, the n of Nagelkerke's formula is the sum of the
weights. Weights that are counts thus give the values of the expanded
data, and non-integer weights give those of the weighted
log-likelihood \code{glm()} maximizes, which do not change when the weights
are rescaled. The \code{n} row still counts rows.

Tjur's \eqn{R^2}{R^2} depends more than McFadden's on the proportion of
events: in a simulated logit with fixed slopes, taking that proportion
from 0.50 to 0.05 moves Tjur's from 0.21 to 0.07 and McFadden's from
0.16 to 0.13. It can decrease when a predictor is added (Tjur 2009,
p. 370; Allison 2014). It is at least 0 for a logit with an intercept,
but a fixed offset can make it negative, and it is shown with its sign.
On request it is also computed for the other links, as the same
difference of means, although the mean predicted probability equals
the proportion of events for a logit with an intercept (Tjur 2009,
p. 366), and not in general under the other links.
}

\section{Multi-model semantics}{
Pass a single fit or a \code{list()} of fits. Multi-model layout
draws a centered \strong{spanner label} above each model's
sub-columns:

\itemize{
\item \code{list("Naive" = m1, "Adjusted" = m2)} -> spanner labels
\code{"Naive"} / \code{"Adjusted"}. Partial naming (\code{list("Naive" = m1, m2)})
auto-fills missing slots as \code{"Model <position>"}.
\item \code{list(m1, m2)} (unnamed) -> if all response variables
differ, each outcome becomes the spanner label of its model
and the redundant Outcome body row is suppressed. The outcome
is named as in the title: its name (from \code{formula(fit)[[2]]}),
or its \code{label} attribute under \code{labels = TRUE}. If DVs match,
the labels default to \code{"Model 1, 2, ..."}.
\item Models with different outcomes that the spanner labels do not
name (named models, or an outcome that several models share) get
an Outcome body row under the headers; \code{outcome_labels = FALSE}
removes it.
\item \code{model_labels = c("A", "B")} overrides everything.
}

A default label is worded in the language of the table
(\code{options(spicy.language)}), over the columns and in the notes that
cite the model; the column names of \code{as.data.frame()} and
\code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}} keep the English one (\code{"Model 1: B"}).

Duplicate explicit names in the list are rejected
(\code{spicy_invalid_input}) -- they would silently collide in the
internal model_id key. So is a name that collides with the
auto-filled label of another slot (\code{list("Model 2" = m1, m2)}):
two models under one spanner cannot be told apart in the table,
nor addressed by \code{\link[=inline]{inline()}}. Rename the model, or pass
\code{model_labels}.
\subsection{Stacked layout}{

\code{layout = "stacked"} sets the models one under the other, one panel
per model, through \code{\link[=table_stack]{table_stack()}}. Each panel is exactly the table
\code{table_regression()} gives that model alone, with the same arguments:
its default columns (\code{"all_b"}, the confidence interval included), its
reference rows, blocks and fit statistics. A row with the label of the
model opens each panel: \code{model_labels}, else the names of \code{models},
else the outcomes when they all differ, else "Model k". The panels
share one column header when they have the same columns, and each opens
with its own header otherwise (an odds ratio beside a coefficient, a
multinomial model with its outcome categories in columns). Models with
different outcomes that the labels of the panels do not name open each
panel on an Outcome row, under its label row (\code{outcome_labels}), as the
tables built alone do in \code{\link[=table_stack]{table_stack()}}. \code{vcov}
and \code{cluster} lists give each panel its own estimator. Under
\code{nested = TRUE}, the change statistics of each
model sit in its own panel, labeled "vs" the model before it. The type
of each model, its standard errors and the adjustment of its p-values
are worded as in the note of the models side by side, each panel named
by its label; the other sentences each open with the labels of the
panels they concern. A warning or an error names a model as the
side-by-side table does. \code{tidy()}, \code{glance()} and \code{output = "long"}
return the rows of the models side by side, under a leading \code{panel}
column, with one exception: under \code{keep} or \code{drop}, a multinomial
model returns the rows its panel shows, selected by its terms as in
its table alone (\code{"age"}),
where side by side the patterns meet terms named with their outcome
(\code{"Student: age"}). \code{output = "data.frame"} binds the data frames of
the panels by rows. A \code{keep}, \code{drop} or \code{show_intercept = FALSE} that
leaves a panel without a coefficient row is an error: side by side,
such a model keeps its fit statistics beside the others.
}
}

\section{Inference and standard errors}{
\code{vcov} selects the variance-covariance estimator:

\itemize{
\item \code{"classical"} -- OLS (lm) / Fisher information (glm).
\item \code{"HC0"} to \code{"HC5"} -- heteroskedasticity-consistent
(via \code{\link[sandwich:vcovHC]{sandwich::vcovHC()}}).
\item \code{"CR0"} to \code{"CR3"} -- cluster-robust with
Satterthwaite-corrected df (via \code{\link[clubSandwich:vcovCR]{clubSandwich::vcovCR()}}).
Requires \code{cluster}.
\item \code{"bootstrap"} -- nonparametric or cluster bootstrap
(\code{boot_n} replicates).
\item \code{"jackknife"} -- leave-one-out / leave-one-cluster-out.
}

For multi-model use, both \code{vcov} and \code{cluster} accept a single
value (recycled to all models) or a list (one per model). The
same fit can appear several times with different estimators to
compare standard errors side-by-side.

Inferential regimes for \code{lm} / \code{glm} (B and AME share the same
regime):
\itemize{
\item \code{classical}, \verb{HC*} -> t with \code{df.residual} (\code{lm}). For
\code{glm}, the test \code{summary()} of the model reports: t on the
residual degrees of freedom when the dispersion is estimated
(gaussian, Gamma, inverse Gaussian, Tweedie,
\code{negative.binomial(theta)} and the quasi families), z otherwise
(binomial, Poisson, \code{MASS::glm.nb}).
\item \code{bootstrap}, \code{jackknife} -> z asymptotic.
\item \code{CR0}-\code{CR3} -> t with \strong{Satterthwaite-corrected df} (B
via \code{\link[clubSandwich:coef_test]{clubSandwich::coef_test()}}; AME via
\code{\link[clubSandwich:linear_contrast]{clubSandwich::linear_contrast()}}; Pustejovsky & Tipton
2018). Under non-linear terms (\code{poly()}, \code{I()}, \code{log()},
\code{splines::ns()}), AME falls back to z-asymptotic with a
\code{spicy_fallback} warning.
}
For other classes the t-vs-z axis follows the estimator's native
reference distribution (e.g. \code{glmmTMB}, survival, ordinal,
\code{betareg}, \code{mlogit} use z; \code{lm}, \code{lme}, \code{lmer}, \code{rms::ols} use t).

A single \code{nnet::multinom} model sets its outcome categories as column
groups; see \link{table_regression_categorical}.
\subsection{Robust SE availability by model class}{

Not every estimator is defined for every class. A robust \code{vcov}
the class cannot honor is refused with \code{spicy_unsupported_vcov},
instead of a model-based result under a robust label. The
values each class accepts, the \code{vcov} column of
\code{\link[=table_regression_models]{table_regression_models()}}:\tabular{lll}{
   Family \tab Class \tab vcov \cr
   Linear and generalized linear \tab \code{lm} \tab classical, HC0-HC5, CR0-CR3, CR1S, bootstrap, jackknife \cr
   Linear and generalized linear \tab \code{glm} \tab classical, HC0-HC5, CR0-CR3, bootstrap, jackknife \cr
   Linear and generalized linear \tab \code{negbin} \tab classical, HC0-HC5, CR0-CR3, bootstrap, jackknife \cr
   Linear and generalized linear \tab \code{rlm} \tab classical \cr
   Linear and generalized linear \tab \code{nls} \tab classical \cr
   Robust, IV, quantile, panel \tab \code{lm_robust} \tab classical (the fit's own) \cr
   Robust, IV, quantile, panel \tab \code{iv_robust} \tab classical (the fit's own) \cr
   Robust, IV, quantile, panel \tab \code{ivreg} \tab classical \cr
   Robust, IV, quantile, panel \tab \code{tobit} \tab classical, CR1 \cr
   Robust, IV, quantile, panel \tab \code{rq} \tab classical (= nid), nid, iid, ker, rank, bootstrap \cr
   Robust, IV, quantile, panel \tab \code{fixest} \tab classical (the fit's own) \cr
   Mixed effects \tab \code{lmerMod} \tab classical, CR0-CR3 \cr
   Mixed effects \tab \code{glmerMod} \tab classical \cr
   Mixed effects \tab \code{glmmTMB} \tab classical, CR0, CR1 (classical only for REML or the ordinal family) \cr
   Mixed effects \tab \code{lme} \tab classical, CR0-CR3 (classical only with contrasts other than the session's) \cr
   Mixed effects \tab \code{gls} \tab classical \cr
   Ordinal \tab \code{polr} \tab classical, CR1 \cr
   Ordinal \tab \code{clm} \tab classical, CR1 (classical only with scale or nominal terms) \cr
   Ordinal \tab \code{clmm} \tab classical \cr
   Categorical \tab \code{multinom} \tab classical, CR1 \cr
   Categorical \tab \code{mlogit} \tab classical, CR1 \cr
   Counts, two-part \tab \code{zeroinfl} \tab classical, CR1 (classical only for a negative-binomial part) \cr
   Counts, two-part \tab \code{hurdle} \tab classical, CR1 (classical only for a negative-binomial part) \cr
   Survival \tab \code{coxph} \tab classical, CR0 \cr
   Survival \tab \code{survreg} \tab classical, CR1 \cr
   Survival \tab \code{cph} \tab classical, CR0 (classical only without x = TRUE, y = TRUE) \cr
   Survival \tab \code{flexsurvreg} \tab classical \cr
   Survey-weighted \tab \code{svyglm} \tab classical (design-based) \cr
   Survey-weighted \tab \code{svyolr} \tab classical (design-based) \cr
   Survey-weighted \tab \code{svycoxph} \tab classical (design-based) \cr
   Population-averaged (GEE) \tab \code{geeglm} \tab classical (the fit's own) \cr
   Additive, proportions, selection \tab \code{gam} \tab classical, CR1 \cr
   Additive, proportions, selection \tab \code{betareg} \tab classical, CR1 \cr
   Additive, proportions, selection \tab \code{selection} \tab classical \cr
   rms \tab \code{ols} \tab classical, CR0 (classical only without x = TRUE, y = TRUE) \cr
   rms \tab \code{lrm} \tab classical, CR0 (classical only without x = TRUE, y = TRUE) \cr
   rms \tab \code{Glm} \tab classical, CR0 (classical only without x = TRUE, y = TRUE) \cr
   Bayesian \tab \code{stanreg} \tab classical (the posterior) \cr
   Bayesian \tab \code{brmsfit} \tab classical (the posterior) \cr
}


\code{"classical"} is the model-based variance, or the variance the fit
carries for the classes marked so: \code{estimatr} and \code{fixest} fits keep
the estimator they were computed with, a GEE fit its own sandwich, a
survey fit its design-based variance, a Bayesian fit its posterior.
Why the other tokens are refused, and the backend of each class, is
on the page of its family (see \emph{Pages by model family}); for \code{lm},
\code{glm}, GEE, \code{gam}, \code{betareg} and the \code{rms} fits, it is below.

\code{lm} also takes \code{"CR1S"}, the full Stata \verb{regress, vce(cluster)} convention: the CR1S small-sample scaling (equal to
\code{\link[sandwich:vcovCL]{sandwich::vcovCL()}} with \code{type = "HC1"}) with \code{t(G - 1)} inference,
\code{G} the number of clusters. Use it to reproduce Stata tables; \code{"CR2"}
(Bell-McCaffrey, Satterthwaite df) remains the recommended modern
choice. \code{"CR1S"} is refused for \code{glm} with an explanation: Stata's ML
commands use a different convention (\code{G/(G-1)} scaling, z), so the
label would not match Stata output there. For \code{MASS::glm.nb}, the
sandwich holds theta at its estimate, so robust standard errors can
differ by a few percent from software that includes the score of
theta.

A GEE fit (\code{geepack::geeglm}) is robust by construction: the fit's
own sandwich (or jackknife) standard errors, chosen by geeglm's
\verb{std.err =} option and clustered on its \verb{id =}, are the displayed
inference. \verb{HC*}, \verb{CR*} and \code{cluster} are refused with a pointer to
those fit options.

Cluster-robust backends differ by class but are each cross-validated
to the field-standard oracle: \code{lm}/\code{glm}/\code{lmer}/\code{lme} use
\pkg{clubSandwich} (CR2 = Bell-McCaffrey, with Satterthwaite df for
\code{lm}/\code{lme}/\code{lmer}); \code{coxph}/\code{cph} use the Lin-Wei grouped-dfbeta
sandwich (identical to \code{coxph(..., cluster=)});
\code{survreg}/\code{tobit}/\code{gam}/\code{polr}/\code{clm}/\code{betareg}/\code{mlogit}/\code{multinom} and the
Poisson and geometric \code{pscl} two-part fits use
\code{\link[sandwich:vcovCL]{sandwich::vcovCL()}}. For \code{gam}/\code{bam}, spicy forms the sandwich
itself, from the GLM score and the penalized covariance of the fit,
and lets \code{vcovCL()} do only the clustering arithmetic: the sandwich
methods for these fits are wrong for a free dispersion, a
non-canonical link and a non-Gaussian \code{bam()}. Extended families
other than \code{nb()} (\code{betar}, \code{ocat}, \code{scat}, \code{tw}, ...) have another
score and are refused. For all of these \code{vcovCL()} classes, the one
estimator is CR1, requested as \code{"CR1"}; the other \verb{CR*} tokens are
refused rather than mapped. \code{rms} fits use
\code{\link[rms:robcov]{rms::robcov()}} (which
needs the fit's \verb{x = TRUE, y = TRUE}). The Lin-Wei and \code{robcov()}
sandwiches have no small-sample factor: their one estimator is
requested as \code{"CR0"}, and the other \verb{CR*} tokens are refused rather
than mapped. \code{glmmTMB} fits use
\code{glmmTMB::sandwich()} (see \link{table_regression_mixed}). \code{cluster} length is one
entry per observation, except \code{mlogit} (one per choice situation)
and censored \code{coxph} (one per subject).
}

\subsection{How to specify \code{cluster}}{

Three accepted forms, in order of preference:

\enumerate{
\item \strong{Formula} -- \code{cluster = ~region} (or
\code{cluster = ~region:year} for the interaction of two
variables). The variables are looked up in
\code{model.frame(fit)} first, then in the original \code{data}
argument captured by the fit. \strong{Recommended}: independent
of the dataset's name, composable for multi-way clustering,
consistent with \code{\link[sandwich:vcovCL]{sandwich::vcovCL()}} /
\code{\link[clubSandwich:vcovCR]{clubSandwich::vcovCR()}}.
\item \strong{String} -- \code{cluster = "region"}. A single column
name resolved the same way as the formula. Convenient but
cannot express interactions.
\item \strong{Vector} -- \code{cluster = df$region}. An atomic vector of
length \code{nobs(fit)}. Use this when the cluster key is
\strong{derived on the fly} (\code{cluster = interaction(df$region, df$year)},
\code{cluster = as.integer(format(df$date, "\%Y"))}), comes from
a \strong{different dataset} with matching row order, or is
otherwise not a column of the model's \code{data}.
}

A bare unquoted name (\code{cluster = region}) is \strong{not} column
selection: it is evaluated as an ordinary R variable. If an object
\code{region} exists in the calling environment, its value is used as
the \strong{vector} form above; if it does not (the typical "unquoted
column name" intent), the call fails with a migration error
pointing at \code{~region} / \code{"region"}. Bare-name column selection is
deliberately unsupported -- it would require non-standard
evaluation magic that breaks under programmatic use (function
wrapping, dynamic column choice, loops).

For multi-model use, mix forms freely:
\code{cluster = list(~region, "region", df$region)}.
}
}

\section{Hierarchical (nested) model comparison}{
\code{nested = TRUE} adds \strong{per-pair change statistics as in-table
rows} (APA Manual 7 Table 7.15, p. 219 / Stata \code{esttab} / SPSS Model Summary
convention). Each adjacent pair (M2 vs M1, M3 vs M2, ...)
contributes one column of change stats; the FIRST model column
gets en-dashes (no previous model to compare to). Validation
requires identical \code{nobs} and identical response variable
across all models.

\strong{Structural refusals.} Four hierarchies carry no valid change
statistic whatever their \code{nobs} and response, and are refused with
\code{spicy_invalid_input} rather than rendered with empty or
meaningless cells:
\itemize{
\item \emph{A REML pair whose fixed effects differ}
(\code{nlme::lme(method = "REML")}, \code{glmmTMB(REML = TRUE)}); see
\link{table_regression_mixed}.
\item \emph{Two \code{fixest} fits that absorbed different fixed
effects}; see \link{table_regression_robust}.
\item \emph{Mixed-effects fits from different engines} (\code{lme4},
\code{glmmTMB}, \code{nlme::lme}); see \link{table_regression_mixed}.
\item \emph{A \code{MASS::rlm} hierarchy}. M-estimation minimizes a
bounded loss on the scaled residuals: it partitions no sums of
squares and it is not a likelihood, so there is neither a
likelihood-ratio test nor a partial F to report. Every change
token is refused -- including on a single fit or a
\code{nested = FALSE} pair, where the token is the explicit
request. Compare the least-squares fits (\code{lm()}) if the
hierarchy itself is the question.
}
In the first three, \code{nested = FALSE} renders the models side by
side instead.

Default change tokens auto-injected when \code{show_fit_stats} is
\code{NULL}:
\itemize{
\item All-lm (and \code{nls}): \code{c("r2_change", "f_change", "p_change")} -- APA hierarchical regression standard.
\item All-glm: \code{c("lrt_change", "p_change")} -- Hosmer,
Lemeshow & Sturdivant 2013 Section 2.4; Long & Freese 2014
Section 3.2.4.
\item All-glm whose dispersion is estimated (gaussian, Gamma,
inverse Gaussian, Tweedie, quasi families), and the \code{feglm}
fits of these families: \code{c("f_change", "p_change")}, the F test
\code{anova(test = "F")} reports, \eqn{F = (\Delta D / q) /
    \hat\phi}{F = (Delta D / q) / phi} on \eqn{(q, df)}, with
\eqn{\hat\phi}{phi} the dispersion and \eqn{df} the residual
degrees of freedom of the larger model. \code{"lrt_change"} is
refused for such a hierarchy.
\item Any other hierarchy that carries a likelihood
(\code{survreg}, \code{polr}, \code{clm}, \code{coxph}, \code{gls}, \code{betareg},
\code{multinom}, \code{fixest}, ...): the same
\code{c("lrt_change", "p_change")}.
\item Mixed-effects: see \link{table_regression_mixed}.
\item Quantile (\code{rq}): \code{c("f_change", "p_change")} -- the
Wald-type test \code{quantreg::anova.rq()} reports.
}
To customize, pass the change tokens directly to
\code{show_fit_stats}. The variance-explained change tokens
(\code{"r2_change"}, \code{"adj_r2_change"}, \code{"f_change"},
\code{"f2_change"}) raise \code{spicy_invalid_input} on any hierarchy
whose nested comparison is a likelihood-ratio test -- \code{glm}
(\code{"f_change"} excepted when its dispersion is estimated),
and equally every other likelihood class and the
mixed-effects families: that comparison reports a chi-square,
not a variance-explained change, and the message points at
\code{"lrt_change"} + \code{"p_change"}. A quantile hierarchy refuses
them too, and \code{"lrt_change"} with them, pointing at
\code{"f_change"} + \code{"p_change"} instead. \code{lm} and \code{nls} keep the
least-squares tokens.

\strong{The change test follows \code{vcov}.} Under the classical \code{vcov} the
change test is the partial F, the likelihood-ratio test, the F test
\code{anova()} reports for a pair whose dispersion is estimated, or
\code{quantreg::anova.rq()}'s Wald test, as above. Under any other
\code{vcov}, and under the classical token when the fit carries a robust
variance of its own, it is the Wald test of the coefficients the
current model adds, on the matrix its coefficient rows were computed
from: \eqn{W = b' V^{-1} b}{W = b' V^-1 b}, with \eqn{b} the \eqn{q}
added coefficients (aliased ones left out) and \eqn{V} their block of
the matrix. The test takes the regime of the current model's
coefficient tests:
\itemize{
\item \emph{F}, in a row labeled "Wald F-change": \eqn{W / q} on
\eqn{(q, df)} degrees of freedom, the convention of
\code{lmtest::waldtest(test = "F")} and \code{car::linearHypothesis()}.
It applies to \code{lm} under \code{"HC0"}-\code{"HC5"} (and \code{"CR1S"}, on
\eqn{G - 1} degrees of freedom), \code{glm} with an estimated
dispersion under \code{"HC0"}-\code{"HC5"}, \code{rms::ols} under \code{"CR0"} or
after \code{rms::robcov()}, \code{quantreg::rq} under \code{"iid"} and
\code{"ker"}, and a fixest fit estimated with a non-iid vcov whose
coefficient tests are t (on fixest's own degrees of freedom, as
\code{fixest::wald()}): \code{feols}, and \code{feglm} with a family whose
dispersion is estimated (gaussian, Gamma, inverse Gaussian and
the quasi families). Under
\code{"CR0"}-\code{"CR3"}, \code{lm}, \code{glm} (\code{MASS::glm.nb} included),
\code{lme4::lmer} and \code{nlme::lme} use the HTZ test of
\code{clubSandwich::Wald_test()}, the small-sample correction of their
coefficient tests.
\item \emph{Chi-square}, in a row labeled "Wald \eqn{\chi^2}{chi^2}
(change)" that replaces the likelihood-ratio row: \eqn{W} on
\eqn{q} degrees of freedom, the convention of
\code{lmtest::waldtest(test = "Chisq")}. It applies to \code{glm} with a
fixed dispersion and \code{glm.nb} under \code{"HC0"}-\code{"HC5"}, \code{lm} and
\code{glm} under
\code{"bootstrap"} and \code{"jackknife"}, \code{rq} under \code{"bootstrap"},
\code{glmmTMB} under \code{"CR0"} and \code{"CR1"},
under \code{"CR0"} to \code{coxph} and \code{rms::lrm} / \code{cph} / \code{Glm},
and under \code{"CR1"} to \code{survreg}, \code{mgcv::gam} and \code{bam}, \code{polr},
\code{clm}, \code{betareg}, \code{multinom}, \code{mlogit}, \code{zeroinfl} and
\code{hurdle} (not negative binomial); also to
\code{survreg(robust = TRUE)}, a fixest fit with a non-iid vcov whose
coefficient tests are z (\code{fepois}, \code{femlm}, \code{fenegbin}, and
\code{feglm} with the binomial or Poisson family), and an \code{rms} fit of
these classes passed through \code{robcov()}.
}
\eqn{\Delta R^2}{Delta R^2}, the information criteria and the
deviance change do not depend on the vcov and are unchanged, and a
\code{vcov} list tests each pair with the current model's estimator. The
previous model's coefficients must all appear, under the same names,
in the current one, and a smooth term added to a \code{gam} under a robust
\code{vcov} is refused (\code{spicy_invalid_input} in both cases). \code{rq} keeps
\code{anova.rq()}'s test under \code{"nid"}, its default, and under \code{"rank"},
which yields no matrix. A Wald test of many constraints in a small
sample is liberal: on 219 observations, a block of 19 predictors gave
F(19, 178) = 6.91 classically and 9.06 as a Wald HC3 test. The HTZ
correction under \code{"CR0"}-\code{"CR3"} mitigates it.
}

\section{Standardized coefficients}{
\code{standardized} controls the method when \code{"beta"} is in
\code{show_columns}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"refit"} -- refit on z-scored data. For \code{lm} both X
and Y are z-scored (Cohen et al. 2003); for
\code{glm} only numeric X (Long & Freese 2014 Section 4.7.2
"x-standardization").
\item \code{"posthoc"} -- post-hoc scaling. lm:
\eqn{\beta = B \times SD(X) / SD(Y)}{beta = B * SD(X) / SD(Y)};
glm: X-only \eqn{\beta = B \times SD(X)}{beta = B * SD(X)}
(Y is undefined on the link scale).
\item \code{"basic"} -- like \code{"posthoc"} but factor dummies are
scaled by their column SD.
\item \code{"smart"} -- Gelman (2008): continuous numeric inputs
are scaled by \code{2 * SD(X)} (a +/-1 SD swing then spans the
same range as a binary's 0 to 1 step); binary inputs --
numeric 0/1 and factor dummies -- are left unscaled. (Before
0.13.0 the rule was applied inverted; see NEWS.)
\item \code{"pseudo"} -- \emph{glm only}. Menard (2004, 2011)
fully-standardized \eqn{\beta = B \times SD(X) / SD(Y^*)}{beta = B * SD(X) / SD(Y*)},
with \eqn{Y^*}{Y*} the latent variable on the link scale and
\eqn{SD(Y^*) = \sqrt{Var(\hat{\eta}) + Var_{link}}}{SD(Y*) = sqrt(Var(eta-hat) + Var_link)}
(\eqn{\pi^2/3}{pi^2/3} logit, \code{1} probit,
\eqn{\pi^2/6}{pi^2/6} cloglog). Binomial families only;
non-binomial returns NA with a \code{spicy_caveat}.
\item \code{"none"} (default) -- no \eqn{\beta}{beta} computed.
}

\strong{Interactions and transforms.} Under \code{"refit"}, an interaction's
\eqn{\beta}{beta} is the coefficient of the product of the z-scored
components -- the recommended treatment for such models (Cohen et
al. 2003 Section 7.5; Aiken & West 1991; Friedrich 1982). Under
\code{"posthoc"}, \code{"basic"}, and \code{"smart"}, the product / transformed
design column is treated as a single numeric column and scaled by
its own SD (under \code{"smart"}, by \code{2 * SD} when the product column is
continuous; a binary product column -- e.g. a binary-by-binary
interaction -- stays unscaled like any binary input) -- the
convention of SPSS beta, Stata \verb{regress, beta}, SAS PROC REG \code{STB}, and
\code{lm.beta::lm.beta()}, identical to
\code{effectsize::standardize_parameters(method = "basic")} on those
columns. The two conventions differ whenever the components are
correlated; a \code{spicy_caveat} warns and the footer names the
convention actually used. \code{"refit"} declines formulas with inline
transforms (\code{log(x)}, \code{poly()}, \code{factor()} written in the formula):
the model frame's evaluated columns cannot be re-evaluated on
z-scored data, so spicy falls back to \code{"posthoc"} with a warning
(\code{effectsize}'s refit instead standardizes \emph{before} the transform
-- a different estimand). Pre-build transformed columns in \code{data}
for an exact refit.
}

\section{Multiple-comparison adjustment}{
Adjusting the p-values of all coefficients of a single
regression model is \strong{not} the standard convention. Each
coefficient tests a distinct hypothesis on a distinct
predictor -- not the situation multiple-testing procedures
were designed for (Rothman 1990; Greenland 2017; see also Gelman,
Hill & Yajima 2012). Hence the default \code{p_adjust = "none"}.

Adjustment is appropriate for: mass screening with no prior
hypothesis (typically \code{"BH"} / FDR), pre-registered
multi-endpoint confirmatory designs (typically \code{"holm"}), or
when a journal / SAP explicitly requests it.

The adjustment runs \strong{before} any \code{keep} / \code{drop} filtering,
so the family is the model's full coefficient set (intercept
and reference rows excluded), not the displayed subset --
filtering is a display choice and must not change the
inferential family.
}

\section{Output formats and broom integration}{
\code{output} selects the return type:
\itemize{
\item \code{"default"} -- a \code{spicy_regression_table}
(\code{data.frame} subclass) printed via \code{\link[=spicy_print_table]{spicy_print_table()}}.
\item \code{"data.frame"} -- the displayed table with its numbers,
\link[=as.data.frame.spicy_regression_table]{as.data.frame()} of the
default object: one row per printed row, the key columns
\code{variable}, \code{label}, \code{level} and \code{.row_role}, then the printed
columns, numeric, each interval split into its two bounds.
\item \code{"long"} -- the long-format tibble, one row per
\verb{(model_id, term, estimate_type)}, with broom's column names.
\item \code{"tinytable"} / \code{"gt"} / \code{"flextable"} -- rich-format
HTML / Word / PDF tables (require the corresponding
Suggests package). In a PDF, a \code{"flextable"} table wider than
the page can be fitted with \code{flextable::fit_to_width()}.
\item \code{"excel"} -- writes to \code{excel_path} via
\code{openxlsx2::wb_save()} and returns the path invisibly.
\item \code{"word"} -- writes to \code{word_path} via
\code{flextable::save_as_docx()} and returns the path invisibly.
\item \code{"clipboard"} -- copies to the system clipboard via
\code{\link[clipr:write_clip]{clipr::write_clip()}}.
}

\code{\link[broom:tidy]{broom::tidy()}} returns a long tibble with one row per
\verb{(model_id, term, estimate_type)} and broom-canonical column
names (\code{estimate}, \code{std.error}, \code{conf.low}, \code{conf.high},
\code{statistic}, \code{p.value}). \code{\link[broom:glance]{broom::glance()}} returns one row per
model with the model-level statistics; \code{df.residual} is kept
numeric so cluster-robust Satterthwaite df is preserved.
}

\section{Global options}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{options(spicy.style = )}} -- the journal style applied by
default to all nine table functions (\code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}},
\code{\link[=table_categorical_svy]{table_categorical_svy()}}, \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}},
\code{\link[=table_continuous_svy]{table_continuous_svy()}}, \code{\link[=table_continuous_lm]{table_continuous_lm()}},
\code{\link[=table_outcome]{table_outcome()}}, \code{\link[=table_multiresponse]{table_multiresponse()}}, \code{table_regression()}
and \code{\link[=table_regression_uv]{table_regression_uv()}}). Takes a theme
name (\code{"jama"}, \code{"nejm"}, \code{"lancet"}, \code{"annals"}, \code{"apa"},
\code{"aer"}) or a \code{\link[=spicy_style]{spicy_style()}} object. The style of a report is a
property of the document, so it is set once in the setup chunk;
the \code{style} argument overrides it per call, and any formatting
argument you type overrides both. \code{options(spicy.style = NULL)}
restores spicy's own defaults. See \code{\link[=spicy_style]{spicy_style()}} for the exact
rules each theme encodes and the official document they come
from.
\item \strong{\code{options(spicy.note_style = )}} -- how the table note is
rendered by the \code{"tinytable"} engine (every table function).
A note is subordinate to the table it documents, so it is set
one size down, in black: \verb{0.9em} (\verb{\\small} in LaTeX), matching
the \code{"gt"} engine and the 10pt-on-11pt of the Word engine. When
\code{tinytable::style_tt(fontsize = )} resizes the whole body, the
note takes 0.9 of that size. Two opt-ins:
\itemize{
\item \code{"none"} -- no intervention; the note is rendered
exactly as the receiving document template styles it.
\item any other string -- extra arguments for the Typst
\code{text()} call around the note, appended to the size (e.g.
\code{options(spicy.note_style = "fill: luma(89)")} for a gray
note). Typst only: the HTML note keeps its size.
}
\code{options(spicy.note_style = NULL)} restores the default.
}
}

\section{Weights}{
No \code{weights} argument: weights are a property of the fit
(extracted via \code{\link[stats:weights]{stats::weights()}}). Pass them when fitting:
\code{lm(y ~ x, data = df, weights = w)}. All downstream
computations (vcov, AME, standardization, \code{weighted_nobs})
extract them automatically.
}

\section{Internationalization}{
Output follows \code{options(spicy.language)} (see \code{\link[=spicy_labels]{spicy_labels()}}); the
column names of \code{as.data.frame()} and \code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}} stay English
in every language. Override user-facing strings via
\code{reference_label}, \code{model_labels}, \code{outcome_labels}, and
\code{labels}. The title and footer are post-processable via
\code{attr(result, "title")} and \code{attr(result, "note")}.
}

\section{Classed conditions}{

Every error and warning emitted by \code{table_regression()} carries
a classed condition for programmatic dispatch via \code{\link[=tryCatch]{tryCatch()}}
or \code{\link[=withCallingHandlers]{withCallingHandlers()}}. Errors inherit from \code{spicy_error}
(root); warnings from \code{spicy_warning}. Specific leaves used by
this function include \code{spicy_invalid_input},
\code{spicy_invalid_data}, \code{spicy_unsupported},
\code{spicy_unsupported_vcov}, \code{spicy_unsupported_standardized},
\code{spicy_missing_pkg}, \code{spicy_missing_column},
\code{spicy_ignored_arg}, \code{spicy_caveat}, \code{spicy_fallback}. See
\code{\link[=spicy-package]{spicy}} for the full taxonomy.
}

\examples{
# ---- Single-model usage ------------------------------------------
fit <- lm(wellbeing_score ~ age + sex + smoking, data = sochealth)

# Default APA layout: B / SE / 95\% CI / p plus the n / R^2 /
# Adj. R^2 fit-stats footer. Factor reference level is annotated
# with `(ref.)` and shows an en dash in the statistic columns.
table_regression(fit)

\donttest{
# Standardized coefficients (beta) injected next to B. "refit"
# is the Cohen et al. (2003) refit-on-z-scores convention;
# "basic" reproduces the SPSS / Stata regress, beta definition.
table_regression(fit, standardized = "refit")
}

\donttest{
# Custom column set: B + AME + AME-specific p-value. Note that
# the `p` token always belongs to B, never to AME -- use the
# explicit `ame_p` token for AME inference.
if (requireNamespace("marginaleffects", quietly = TRUE)) {
  table_regression(
    fit,
    show_columns = c("b", "p", "ame", "ame_ci", "ame_p")
  )
}
}

\donttest{
# Group-token shortcut: "all_b" + "all_ame" expands to the full
# B / AME column families side by side.
if (requireNamespace("marginaleffects", quietly = TRUE)) {
  table_regression(fit, show_columns = c("all_b", "all_ame"))
}
}

# ---- Cluster-robust variance -------------------------------------
# CR2 (Bell-McCaffrey) with Satterthwaite-corrected df is the
# recommended default under few clusters. Three forms are accepted
# for `cluster`; the formula is preferred for composability with
# multi-way clustering and for programmatic robustness.
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("clubSandwich", quietly = TRUE)) {
  table_regression(fit, vcov = "CR2", cluster = ~region)
}
if (requireNamespace("clubSandwich", quietly = TRUE)) {
  table_regression(fit, vcov = "CR2", cluster = "region")
}
if (requireNamespace("clubSandwich", quietly = TRUE)) {
  table_regression(fit, vcov = "CR2", cluster = ~region:age_group)
}
}

\donttest{
# ---- Hierarchical (nested) regression ----------------------------
# Adds in-table change-statistic rows (Delta R^2 / F-change /
# p-change for lm; LRT / p-change for glm) below the fit-stats.
# Note: hierarchical comparison requires identical observations
# across all models -- prepare a complete-case subset first so
# R's listwise deletion does not produce different `nobs` per
# model (which the function rejects).
sochealth_cc <- na.omit(
  sochealth[, c("wellbeing_score", "age", "sex", "smoking")]
)
m1 <- lm(wellbeing_score ~ age,                  data = sochealth_cc)
m2 <- lm(wellbeing_score ~ age + sex,            data = sochealth_cc)
m3 <- lm(wellbeing_score ~ age + sex + smoking,  data = sochealth_cc)
table_regression(
  list("Step 1" = m1, "Step 2" = m2, "Step 3" = m3),
  nested = TRUE
)

# The same comparison by its fit statistics alone.
table_regression(
  list("Step 1" = m1, "Step 2" = m2, "Step 3" = m3),
  show_columns = "none",
  nested = TRUE
)
}

\donttest{
# ---- Side-by-side variance comparison ----------------------------
# Same fit, three vcovs in one wide table. Useful for showing the
# sensitivity of inference to the variance assumption.
if (requireNamespace("clubSandwich", quietly = TRUE)) {
  table_regression(
    list("Classical" = fit, "HC3" = fit, "CR2" = fit),
    vcov    = list("classical", "HC3", "CR2"),
    cluster = list(NULL, NULL, ~region)
  )
}
}

# ---- Tidy long format for downstream pipelines -------------------
if (requireNamespace("broom", quietly = TRUE)) {
  broom::tidy(table_regression(fit))
}

\dontrun{
# ---- Rich-format outputs (require optional Suggests packages) ----
table_regression(fit, output = "gt")
table_regression(fit, output = "flextable")
table_regression(fit, output = "tinytable")

# ---- File outputs ------------------------------------------------
table_regression(fit, output = "excel",
                 excel_path = tempfile(fileext = ".xlsx"))
table_regression(fit, output = "word",
                 word_path  = tempfile(fileext = ".docx"))

# ---- System clipboard (interactive use) --------------------------
table_regression(fit, output = "clipboard")
}

}
